@@ -0,0 +1,111 @@
+# Cluster Checkpoint & Artifact Naming
+
+This is the canonical naming convention for Cluster source checkpoints and generated artifacts from WIP57 P16B forward. The active checkpoint advances independently; examples are not release-state authority.
+
+## Identity layers
+
+Cluster uses four separate identities. They must not be collapsed into one giant filename-shaped version number.
+
+1. **Product version** — semantic product version, for example `v0.505.0`.
+2. **Release train** — prerelease/release-candidate train, for example `RC17`.
+3. **Work checkpoint** — bounded internal workstream, for example `WIP57`.
+4. **Implementation pass** — ordered slice inside the work checkpoint, for example `P16B`.
+
+Human display form:
+
+`v0.505.0 · RC17 · WIP57 · P16C`
+
+Machine checkpoint ID:
+
+`0.505.0-rc.17+work.wip57.p16c`
+
+The current runtime/version-gating contract continues to advertise product/API/schema versions separately during RC17. Do not change protocol compatibility semantics merely to make a filename prettier.
+
+## Pass notation
+
+Use `P<number>` for a normal pass and `P<number><letter>` when a pass has deliberately bounded sub-slices.
+
+Examples:
+
+- `P16`
+- `P16A`
+- `P16B`
+- `P17`
+
+Do not mix `Pass16A`, `Pass-16A`, `pass16a`, and `Bugfix-Pass-16A` in artifact names.
+
+## Artifact filenames
+
+Canonical order:
+
+`<Product>-v<version>-<train>-<wip>-<pass>-<scope>-<role>.<ext>`
+
+Use short scope slugs. The filename identifies the artifact; release notes explain the novel.
+
+### Full reconstructed/master source
+
+`Cluster-Master-v0.505.0-RC17-WIP57-P16C-Capability-Exposure-Closeout.zip`
+
+### Runnable Windows lean package
+
+`Cluster-v0.505.0-RC17-WIP57-P16C-Capability-Exposure-Closeout-Windows-Lean.zip`
+
+### Transplant overlay
+
+`Cluster-v0.505.0-RC17-WIP57-P16C-Capability-Exposure-Closeout-Overlay.zip`
+
+### Patch
+
+`Cluster-v0.505.0-RC17-WIP57-P16C-Capability-Exposure-Closeout.patch`
+
+### Handoff
+
+`Cluster-v0.505.0-RC17-WIP57-P16C-to-P17-Handoff.md`
+
+
+## Active checkpoint versus historical gates
+
+`VERSION_NAMING_CONVENTION.json.currentCheckpoint` is the only naming record that advances with active engineering work. Historical assertion gates must reference `certifiedExamples` or their own immutable expected checkpoint IDs. Advancing from `P16B` to `P16C` must never make the P16B regression gate fail merely because time continued to pass.
+
+## Retired filename habits
+
+From P16B forward, new artifacts should not introduce:
+
+- `Phase10` in filenames. Phase 10 remains historical roadmap context, not checkpoint identity.
+- duplicated `RC17-WIP57` segments.
+- prose such as `Bugfix`, `Closeout`, `Reconstructed`, and `Pass` stacked repeatedly when a short scope already identifies the artifact.
+- ambiguous bare `Pass16` when the actual checkpoint is `P16A` or `P16B`.
+- `(1)`, `(2)`, `(3)` copy suffixes as intentional release identity.
+
+Historical packages keep their historical names. Do not rename old signed/hashed artifacts just to satisfy the new convention.
+
+## Product version transition rule
+
+When Cluster advances from `0.505.x` into the next roadmap product version, keep product versioning and internal checkpoint identity separate:
+
+- product/runtime compatibility: semantic version + protocol/API/schema contracts;
+- release train: `DEV`, `ALPHA`, `BETA`, `RC`, then stable;
+- WIP/pass: engineering checkpoint metadata only.
+
+A future prerelease may therefore expose a SemVer-compatible machine identity such as:
+
+`0.506.0-alpha.1+work.wip1.p01`
+
+while UI can present the calmer human form:
+
+`v0.506.0 Alpha 1 · WIP1 · P01`
+
+## Packaging rule
+
+Every newly packaged checkpoint must record:
+
+- product version;
+- release train;
+- WIP;
+- pass;
+- machine checkpoint ID;
+- source/baseline artifact and SHA-256;
+- artifact role;
+- runtime certification state.
+
+The package filename, build metadata, release notes, manifest, SHA file, and handoff must all agree before the checkpoint can be called trusted.
